WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.120
Since when does Spider-Man have a thing for&nbsp;
curing evil? And is getting into college really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.680
such a big deal for one of the world's most&nbsp;
famous heroes? These questions and more are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.360
the kinds of things in "Spider-Man: No&nbsp;
Way Home" that only adults will notice.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.600
In "Spider-Man: No Way Home," we pick&nbsp;
up right where the last movie left off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.880
with Mysterio revealing Spiderman's identity&nbsp;
as 17 year old high schooler, Peter Parker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:28.160
With so much pressure from the press, police,&nbsp;
and the public, Peter finds his world upended.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.000
Luckily he is cleared of all charges,&nbsp;
but Spider-Man and his best friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.280
MJ and Ned are rejected from MIT due&nbsp;
to their association with Peter Parker.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.560
It's a crushing blow for Peter, that causes him&nbsp;
to seek out Dr. Strange's help and set the plot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:48.320
the whole movie in motion. But the more seasoned&nbsp;
members of the audience might ask: who cares?

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.080
For one thing, MIT's reluctance to&nbsp;
admit Peter Parker and his friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.400
is kind of unrealistic – and also, the&nbsp;
older you get, the more you realize how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.880
overblown the importance of college is.&nbsp;
Sure, higher education is nice and all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.680
and it can get you a leg up in life, but is it&nbsp;
really worth risking the entire universe over?

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.640
As "the most famous person in the world," as well&nbsp;
as a certified genius who's helped save the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.240
Peter could have easily gone into business&nbsp;
with his equally brilliant friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.280
hired world class tutors, or found independent&nbsp;
funding based on his web serum alone!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:25.840
College only gets you so far. Being Spider-Man is&nbsp;
more than enough to get Peter to MIT and beyond.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:30.960
Let's face it – you don't go to a Spider-Man movie&nbsp;
to have a long think about moral relativism. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.000
go for the action, and if you're lucky, a little&nbsp;
bit of heart. In this movie, a bunch of proven bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.280
guys from other universes get sucked into the MCU,&nbsp;
and instead of kicking their butts back to where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.880
they came from, Peter Parker decides to convince&nbsp;
them to change their wicked ways instead. What?

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.200
Why should we be rooting for this? The idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.720
Spider-Man is too good of a guy to&nbsp;
let any harm come to his enemies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:57.680
is kind of childish, not to mention a little&nbsp;
contradictory with movies we've already seen.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.120
"Activate instant kill!"

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.880
It's established in "No Way Home" that the&nbsp;
presence of these villains threatens Peter's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:08.000
universe, and the villains also pose a real danger&nbsp;
to everyone around them. And it sort of undercuts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.960
our years of experience watching this Spider-Man&nbsp;
and others battle various villains to the death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.680
when apparently they could have just done some&nbsp;
vague science stuff and "cured" them all along.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.832
"You deserve that, you're a&nbsp;
criminal! Bye Mr. Criminal!"

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.560
By far the most fun part of&nbsp;
No Way Home is the hilarious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.600
stuttering interplay between the&nbsp;
three versions of Spider-Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.960
all of which appear to have aged along&nbsp;
with the actors that play their characters.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:35.120
Tobey Maguire's Peter has mellowed into&nbsp;
early middle age, Andrew Garfield's Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:39.440
is dealing with the more existential onset of&nbsp;
your 30s, and Tom Holland's Peter needs their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.680
help to deal with some late teenage rage.&nbsp;
As they work on scientific villain cures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.360
and plan the big battle together, there&nbsp;
are enough laughs to fill a whole sitcom.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.680
Older movie goers also saw a bit of themselves&nbsp;
when Garfield and Maguire's Peters Parker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.080
bonded over back pain. Back pain happens&nbsp;
to pretty much everybody, and it's oddly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.960
comforting to see two iterations of Spider-Man&nbsp;
himself complain about "middle-back stuff."

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:05.440
It's enough to make a person grateful that their&nbsp;
biggest issue is finding the right standing desk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.360
or making time for a yoga class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.360
and not constantly sustaining body-blows&nbsp;
from a super-powered rogues' gallery.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.760
You don't have to have seen every previous&nbsp;
Spider-Man movie to enjoy Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.600
No Way Home. But for each movie that you&nbsp;
haven't seen, or don't remember well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.240
there's definitely going to&nbsp;
be a few things you'll miss.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:26.560
This is in addition to the many MCU movies&nbsp;
that No Way Home assumes you've already seen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:30.400
You need that background to understand&nbsp;
the dynamic between Peter and Dr. Strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.080
and so on. But taken as a whole, it's&nbsp;
a lot to ask of the average viewer.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.560
At this point, how many people who saw The&nbsp;
Amazing Spider-Man 2 will remember the finer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:44.000
points of the history between Electro and Andrew&nbsp;
Garfield's Spider-Man? And after fourteen years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.120
we might need a little more than&nbsp;
a line or two's worth of refresher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.800
on what Sandman's whole deal with&nbsp;
his daughter was in Spider-Man 3.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.440
Obviously, the MCU was built on a new strategy&nbsp;
of connecting the movies in a larger universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.360
and it's worked to great effect – but each&nbsp;
installment still has to serve as its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:04.800
story. No Way Home depends almost entirely on your&nbsp;
knowledge of other movies, to a dizzying extent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.120
Kids who have been keeping up will have a blast,&nbsp;
but any adult who's been on-and-off on Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.120
will have a seriously hard time keeping up&nbsp;
with the subtext of what's even happening.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.800
It's one thing for movies to bank on your&nbsp;
nostalgia, but it's another thing to make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:21.600
viewer feel left out, like they haven't done their&nbsp;
homework. If you're a viewer who's getting older&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.600
and maybe started to check out of comic&nbsp;
book movies, Spider-Man: No Way Home is like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:31.520
expert-level nostalgia, designed to challenge your&nbsp;
memory of about a dozen movies from decades past.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:37.280
"So you know too huh? It's cool. I've known first&nbsp;
and I've known longer… It's not a competition."

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.800
If there's one crime the MCU has committed,&nbsp;
it's the popularizing of mid-and-post-credits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.680
scenes. After four phases and two dozen movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.280
Marvel Studios has us trained en masse&nbsp;
to wait through all the credits for one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.360
or two more glimpses of what the future&nbsp;
installments in the series have to offer.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:57.600
Spider-Man: No Way Home has a mid-credits&nbsp;
scene with a fun cameo that's worth the wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:02.400
but then makes you wait alllll the way until&nbsp;
the end for a teaser trailer for Dr. Strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:07.280
in the Multiverse of Madness. It's not even a&nbsp;
post-credits scene! Cool footage or not, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:12.240
just an advertisement. Even by the logic of the&nbsp;
MCU, it seems criminal to make people wait until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.960
the very end of "No Way Home" to see a trailer&nbsp;
for a movie that they'll definitely see anyway.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:19.040
"I'm going to go, thanks so&nbsp;
much everybody for coming!"

